WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.695
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.695 --> 00:00:05.579
I help some educators with
their TCP window sizes.

00:00:05.579 --> 00:00:07.620
Will their networking
performance make the grade?

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:08.740
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.410
[ELECTRONIC MUSIC]

00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:13.640
Tutorama is a platform,
which provides

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:16.680
crowdsourced instructional
videos to help teach people how

00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:18.510
to do everything
from properly walking

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:20.970
a dog to filing their taxes.

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:23.215
In order to handle a massive
spike in video content,

00:00:23.215 --> 00:00:24.840
they recently upgraded
their connection

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:28.200
between their on-prem setup
and Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.250
But despite their
fancy new connection,

00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:32.070
their performance
didn't seem to improve

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.730
in the way they expected.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:35.760
Running iPerf between
their local servers

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.390
and a GCP instance was only
getting about 1.1 megabits

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.070
a second.

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.790
Now we've already
seen that this is

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:45.610
way below what GCP can support
in terms of transport speeds.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.270
So we went down the list,
checking their core account,

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:51.150
if they were using the
right IP, and what regions

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:52.200
they were optimizing for.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.390
And by the way, if you want
to run those same tests,

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.940
check out the videos
linked in the description.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:00.090
Sadly, none of these things had
an impact on their performance.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.090
As such, I took this problem to
a few of our networking gurus.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.640
And after some
thought and debate,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.770
they all pointed me
in the same direction,

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:10.500
to something called
bandwidth delay product.

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.830
See, TCP protocol uses
specific window sizes

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:15.690
that define how
much data to send

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.660
between endpoints before
needing an acknowledgement.

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.610
When your endpoints are
physically close to each other,

00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:23.740
this information can be
sending knowledge quickly.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:26.239
But as that distance
increases, so does

00:01:26.239 --> 00:01:27.780
the time it takes
for the destination

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.940
to acknowledge a window
of data, since we end up

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.500
getting capped by physics.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.780
But here's the important part.

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:35.820
Any time an endpoint
is forced to wait

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.440
for an acknowledgement
of a packet,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:39.300
it creates gaps
in the data flow,

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.580
which then limits the
performance of the connection.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:44.610
As such, it's really important
to fine-tune the TCP window

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.550
size of a connection so that
either side doesn't end up

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.960
waiting for these situations.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.500
Now to be fair,
most modern OSes do

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:55.590
a good job of auto-tuning
the TCP buffer sizes.

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.920
But in some cases, the default
values are still too small.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.070
And as it turns out, the
Tutorama networking engineers

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:03.930
had already adjusted
these windows

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.300
based on their previous
connection speeds.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.460
So to figure out how the
new setup was performing

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.740
by default, we set up a
test from their system

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:14.340
without any adjusted windows,
and saw a whopping 90 megabits

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.010
per second for
external IP access.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.290
From there, we used a
few online calculators

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.110
to try to find better
values for the TCP windows.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:24.810
But quite frankly, the
defaults for the TCP instances

00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:27.270
produced the best results
for the Tutorama team.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.490
At this point, the only
advice I could give them was

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:30.790
don't touch the defaults.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.720
Now I don't say that very
often, but a 90x improvement

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.979
is really hard to beat.

00:02:35.979 --> 00:02:37.770
If you'd like more
details on this problem,

00:02:37.770 --> 00:02:40.111
check out the full article
in the description below.

00:02:40.111 --> 00:02:42.360
And make sure to subscribe
to our Google Cloud YouTube

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:45.370
channel to get alerts when new
performance videos come out.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.410
And don't forget, when
it comes to performance,

00:02:47.410 --> 00:02:48.918
every millisecond counts.

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:53.156
